<DocsPage
  title="Troubleshooting"
  description="The things that go wrong most often, what they usually mean, and the fastest way to confirm it."
  currentPath="/docs/troubleshooting/"
  next={next}
>
  <h2 id="agent-unavailable">Agent Unavailable</h2>
  <p>
    An agent cannot be tagged, or its runs fail immediately. Almost always the
    engine rather than Molt Code:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The CLI is not installed</strong>, or not on the <code>PATH</code> Molt Code sees. Run it directly in a terminal to confirm.</li>
    <li><strong>The CLI is not authenticated</strong> — logins expire. Re-authenticate with the CLI's own command.</li>
    <li><strong>The model is unavailable</strong> to your account, or was retired. Pick another on the agent.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="tag-no-reply">A Tag Produced No Reply</h2>
  <p>
    Tagging always inserts a reply — even a run that fails before it starts
    appears as a failed reply with the error attached. So genuinely nothing
    appearing means the tag did not register:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The handle did not resolve.</strong> An <code>@name</code> that matches no agent stays inert text. Check the spelling against the roster; handles are exact.</li>
    <li><strong>You edited the mention after inserting it.</strong> Re-pick the agent from the composer.</li>
    <li><strong>An agent tagged another agent.</strong> Agent replies never trigger runs — this is deliberate, so agents cannot set each other off in a loop. Tag from your own post.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="permission-blocked">Permission Blocked</h2>
  <p>
    An agent stopped and says it cannot do something:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>It is waiting on you.</strong> Under a stricter permission mode the agent asks before consequential actions. Approve or deny and it continues.</li>
    <li><strong>Its sandbox forbids it.</strong> Adjust the agent's sandbox mode — the setting is on the agent, not the thread.</li>
    <li><strong>It tried to escape the project.</strong> File artifacts must be project-relative, and an agent cannot attach another project's issues. These are enforced; the fix is to work in the right project, or use a <code>#project</code> chip to run there.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>See <a href="/docs/permissions/">Permissions &amp; Safety</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="process-stuck">A Process Is Stuck</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Check the logs first</strong> — <code>moltcode processes logs &lt;id&gt;</code>. A process waiting on a port or a missing environment variable says so.</li>
    <li><strong>Restart it</strong> — <code>moltcode processes restart &lt;id&gt;</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>An agent turn that never ends</strong> usually means a blocking command that does not exit — a dev server started in the foreground. Start long-lived work as a process or in a terminal instead.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="artifact-missing">An Artifact Is Missing or Will Not Open</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The agent described work but attached nothing.</strong> Artifacts are explicit — never scraped from prose. If an agent mentions a file it did not attach, the artifact genuinely is not there. Ask it to attach the reference.</li>
    <li><strong>A file artifact will not open.</strong> That is by design: file artifacts show the path for reference rather than opening in the feed. Use the project's Files or Git tooling.</li>
    <li><strong>An attachment was rejected.</strong> One bad reference rejects the whole list, so an agent that got one path wrong may have attached nothing at all. The reply will say so.</li>
    <li><strong>Only a paperclip and a count is showing.</strong> That is the timeline view. Open the thread for the tiles themselves.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="collecting-logs">Collecting Logs</h2>
  <p>
    When reporting a problem, the useful evidence is usually:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The session behind the reply</strong> — open it from the agent's post. It has the full run: every tool call, in order, with the failure.</li>
    <li><strong>The error message on a failed reply</strong>, verbatim.</li>
    <li><strong>Process or script logs</strong>, if the failure involved one.</li>
    <li><strong>The engine and model</strong> the agent was configured with.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    That set answers most questions immediately. See
    <a href="/docs/support/">Support &amp; Contact</a> for where to send it.
  </p>
</DocsPage>
